    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    Examples[39;49;00m
    [33m    --------[39;49;00m
    [33m    Let us consider the problem of minimizing the Rosenbrock function. This[39;49;00m
    [33m    function (and its respective derivatives) is implemented in `rosen`[39;49;00m
    [33m    (resp. `rosen_der`, `rosen_hess`) in the `scipy.optimize`.[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    >>> from scipy.optimize import minimize, rosen, rosen_der[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    A simple application of the *Nelder-Mead* method is:[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    >>> x0 = [1.3, 0.7, 0.8, 1.9, 1.2][39;49;00m
    [33m    >>> res = minimize(rosen, x0, method='Nelder-Mead', tol=1e-6)[39;49;00m
    [33m    >>> res.x[39;49;00m
    [33m    array([ 1.,  1.,  1.,  1.,  1.])[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    Now using the *BFGS* algorithm, using the first derivative and a few[39;49;00m
    [33m    options:[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    >>> res = minimize(rosen, x0, method='BFGS', jac=rosen_der,[39;49;00m
    [33m    ...                options={'gtol': 1e-6, 'disp': True})[39;49;00m
    [33m    Optimization terminated successfully.[39;49;00m
    [33m             Current function value: 0.000000[39;49;00m
    [33m             Iterations: 26[39;49;00m
    [33m             Function evaluations: 31[39;49;00m
    [33m             Gradient evaluations: 31[39;49;00m
    [33m    >>> res.x[39;49;00m
    [33m    array([ 1.,  1.,  1.,  1.,  1.])[39;49;00m
    [33m    >>> print(res.message)[39;49;00m
    [33m    Optimization terminated successfully.[39;49;00m
    [33m    >>> res.hess_inv[39;49;00m
    [33m    array([[ 0.00749589,  0.01255155,  0.02396251,  0.04750988,  0.09495377],  # may vary[39;49;00m
    [33m           [ 0.01255155,  0.02510441,  0.04794055,  0.09502834,  0.18996269],[39;49;00m
    [33m           [ 0.02396251,  0.04794055,  0.09631614,  0.19092151,  0.38165151],[39;49;00m
    [33m           [ 0.04750988,  0.09502834,  0.19092151,  0.38341252,  0.7664427 ],[39;49;00m
    [33m           [ 0.09495377,  0.18996269,  0.38165151,  0.7664427,   1.53713523]])[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    Next, consider a minimization problem with several constraints (namely[39;49;00m
    [33m    Example 16.4 from [5]_). The objective function is:[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    >>> fun = lambda x: (x[0] - 1)**2 + (x[1] - 2.5)**2[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    There are three constraints defined as:[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    >>> cons = ({'type': 'ineq', 'fun': lambda x:  x[0] - 2 * x[1] + 2},[39;49;00m
    [33m    ...         {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': lambda x: -x[0] - 2 * x[1] + 6},[39;49;00m
    [33m    ...         {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': lambda x: -x[0] + 2 * x[1] + 2})[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    And variables must be positive, hence the following bounds:[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    >>> bnds = ((0, None), (0, None))[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    The optimization problem is solved using the SLSQP method as:[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    >>> res = minimize(fun, (2, 0), method='SLSQP', bounds=bnds,[39;49;00m
    [33m    ...                constraints=cons)[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    It should converge to the theoretical solution (1.4 ,1.7).[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
