    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m            - `LinearConstraint`[39;49;00m
    [33m            - `NonlinearConstraint`[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m        Constraints for COBYLA, SLSQP are defined as a list of dictionaries.[39;49;00m
    [33m        Each dictionary with fields:[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m            type : str[39;49;00m
    [33m                Constraint type: 'eq' for equality, 'ineq' for inequality.[39;49;00m
    [33m            fun : callable[39;49;00m
    [33m                The function defining the constraint.[39;49;00m
    [33m            jac : callable, optional[39;49;00m
    [33m                The Jacobian of `fun` (only for SLSQP).[39;49;00m
    [33m            args : sequence, optional[39;49;00m
    [33m                Extra arguments to be passed to the function and Jacobian.[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m        Equality constraint means that the constraint function result is to[39;49;00m
    [33m        be zero whereas inequality means that it is to be non-negative.[39;49;00m
    [33m        Note that COBYLA only supports inequality constraints.[39;49;00m
    [33m    tol : float, optional[39;49;00m
    [33m        Tolerance for termination. When `tol` is specified, the selected[39;49;00m
    [33m        minimization algorithm sets some relevant solver-specific tolerance(s)[39;49;00m
    [33m        equal to `tol`. For detailed control, use solver-specific[39;49;00m
    [33m        options.[39;49;00m
    [33m    options : dict, optional[39;49;00m
    [33m        A dictionary of solver options. All methods except `TNC` accept the[39;49;00m
    [33m        following generic options:[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m            maxiter : int[39;49;00m
    [33m                Maximum number of iterations to perform. Depending on the[39;49;00m
    [33m                method each iteration may use several function evaluations.[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m                For `TNC` use `maxfun` instead of `maxiter`.[39;49;00m
    [33m            disp : bool[39;49;00m
    [33m                Set to True to print convergence messages.[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m        For method-specific options, see :func:`show_options()`.[39;49;00m
    [33m    callback : callable, optional[39;49;00m
    [33m        A callable called after each iteration.[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m        All methods except TNC, SLSQP, and COBYLA support a callable with[39;49;00m
    [33m        the signature:[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m            ``callback(OptimizeResult: intermediate_result)``[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m        where ``intermediate_result`` is a keyword parameter containing an[39;49;00m
    [33m        `OptimizeResult` with attributes ``x`` and ``fun``, the present values[39;49;00m
    [33m        of the parameter vector and objective function. Note that the name[39;49;00m
    [33m        of the parameter must be ``intermediate_result`` for the callback[39;49;00m
    [33m        to be passed an `OptimizeResult`. These methods will also terminate if[39;49;00m
    [33m        the callback raises ``StopIteration``.[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m        All methods except trust-constr (also) support a signature like:[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m            ``callback(xk)``[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m        where ``xk`` is the current parameter vector.[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m        Introspection is used to determine which of the signatures above to[39;49;00m
    [33m        invoke.[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    Returns[39;49;00m
    [33m    -------[39;49;00m
    [33m    res : OptimizeResult[39;49;00m
    [33m        The optimization result represented as a ``OptimizeResult`` object.[39;49;00m
    [33m        Important attributes are: ``x`` the solution array, ``success`` a[39;49;00m
    [33m        Boolean flag indicating if the optimizer exited successfully and[39;49;00m
    [33m        ``message`` which describes the cause of the termination. See[39;49;00m
    [33m        `OptimizeResult` for a description of other attributes.[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    See also[39;49;00m
    [33m    --------[39;49;00m
    [33m    minimize_scalar : Interface to minimization algorithms for scalar[39;49;00m
    [33m        univariate functions[39;49;00m
    [33m    show_options : Additional options accepted by the solvers[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    Notes[39;49;00m
    [33m    -----[39;49;00m
    [33m    This section describes the available solvers that can be selected by the[39;49;00m
    [33m    'method' parameter. The default method is *BFGS*.[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    **Unconstrained minimization**[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    Method :ref:`CG <optimize.minimize-cg>` uses a nonlinear conjugate[39;49;00m
    [33m    gradient algorithm by Polak and Ribiere, a variant of the[39;49;00m
    [33m    Fletcher-Reeves method described in [5]_ pp.120-122. Only the[39;49;00m
    [33m    first derivatives are used.[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    Method :ref:`BFGS <optimize.minimize-bfgs>` uses the quasi-Newton[39;49;00m
    [33m    method of Broyden, Fletcher, Goldfarb, and Shanno (BFGS) [5]_[39;49;00m
    [33m    pp. 136. It uses the first derivatives only. BFGS has proven good[39;49;00m
    [33m    performance even for non-smooth optimizations. This method also[39;49;00m
    [33m    returns an approximation of the Hessian inverse, stored as[39;49;00m
    [33m    `hess_inv` in the OptimizeResult object.[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    Method :ref:`Newton-CG <optimize.minimize-newtoncg>` uses a[39;49;00m
    [33m    Newton-CG algorithm [5]_ pp. 168 (also known as the truncated[39;49;00m
    [33m    Newton method). It uses a CG method to the compute the search[39;49;00m
    [33m    direction. See also *TNC* method for a box-constrained[39;49;00m
    [33m    minimization with a similar algorithm. Suitable for large-scale[39;49;00m
    [33m    problems.[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    Method :ref:`dogleg <optimize.minimize-dogleg>` uses the dog-leg[39;49;00m
    [33m    trust-region algorithm [5]_ for unconstrained minimization. This[39;49;00m
    [33m    algorithm requires the gradient and Hessian; furthermore the[39;49;00m
    [33m    Hessian is required to be positive definite.[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    Method :ref:`trust-ncg <optimize.minimize-trustncg>` uses the[39;49;00m
    [33m    Newton conjugate gradient trust-region algorithm [5]_ for[39;49;00m
    [33m    unconstrained minimization. This algorithm requires the gradient[39;49;00m
    [33m    and either the Hessian or a function that computes the product of[39;49;00m
    [33m    the Hessian with a given vector. Suitable for large-scale problems.[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    Method :ref:`trust-krylov <optimize.minimize-trustkrylov>` uses[39;49;00m
    [33m    the Newton GLTR trust-region algorithm [14]_, [15]_ for unconstrained[39;49;00m
    [33m    minimization. This algorithm requires the gradient[39;49;00m
    [33m    and either the Hessian or a function that computes the product of[39;49;00m
    [33m    the Hessian with a given vector. Suitable for large-scale problems.[39;49;00m
    [33m    On indefinite problems it requires usually less iterations than the[39;49;00m
    [33m    `trust-ncg` method and is recommended for medium and large-scale problems.[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    Method :ref:`trust-exact <optimize.minimize-trustexact>`[39;49;00m
    [33m    is a trust-region method for unconstrained minimization in which[39;49;00m
    [33m    quadratic subproblems are solved almost exactly [13]_. This[39;49;00m
    [33m    algorithm requires the gradient and the Hessian (which is[39;49;00m
    [33m    *not* required to be positive definite). It is, in many[39;49;00m
    [33m    situations, the Newton method to converge in fewer iterations[39;49;00m
    [33m    and the most recommended for small and medium-size problems.[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    **Bound-Constrained minimization**[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    Method :ref:`Nelder-Mead <optimize.minimize-neldermead>` uses the[39;49;00m
    [33m    Simplex algorithm [1]_, [2]_. This algorithm is robust in many[39;49;00m
    [33m    applications. However, if numerical computation of derivative can be[39;49;00m
    [33m    trusted, other algorithms using the first and/or second derivatives[39;49;00m
    [33m    information might be preferred for their better performance in[39;49;00m
    [33m    general.[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    Method :ref:`L-BFGS-B <optimize.minimize-lbfgsb>` uses the L-BFGS-B[39;49;00m
    [33m    algorithm [6]_, [7]_ for bound constrained minimization.[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    Method :ref:`Powell <optimize.minimize-powell>` is a modification[39;49;00m
    [33m    of Powell's method [3]_, [4]_ which is a conjugate direction[39;49;00m
    [33m    method. It performs sequential one-dimensional minimizations along[39;49;00m
    [33m    each vector of the directions set (`direc` field in `options` and[39;49;00m
    [33m    `info`), which is updated at each iteration of the main[39;49;00m
    [33m    minimization loop. The function need not be differentiable, and no[39;49;00m
    [33m    derivatives are taken. If bounds are not provided, then an[39;49;00m
    [33m    unbounded line search will be used. If bounds are provided and[39;49;00m
    [33m    the initial guess is within the bounds, then every function[39;49;00m
    [33m    evaluation throughout the minimization procedure will be within[39;49;00m
    [33m    the bounds. If bounds are provided, the initial guess is outside[39;49;00m
    [33m    the bounds, and `direc` is full rank (default has full rank), then[39;49;00m
    [33m    some function evaluations during the first iteration may be[39;49;00m
    [33m    outside the bounds, but every function evaluation after the first[39;49;00m
    [33m    iteration will be within the bounds. If `direc` is not full rank,[39;49;00m
    [33m    then some parameters may not be optimized and the solution is not[39;49;00m
    [33m    guaranteed to be within the bounds.[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    Method :ref:`TNC <optimize.minimize-tnc>` uses a truncated Newton[39;49;00m
    [33m    algorithm [5]_, [8]_ to minimize a function with variables subject[39;49;00m
    [33m    to bounds. This algorithm uses gradient information; it is also[39;49;00m
    [33m    called Newton Conjugate-Gradient. It differs from the *Newton-CG*[39;49;00m
    [33m    method described above as it wraps a C implementation and allows[39;49;00m
    [33m    each variable to be given upper and lower bounds.[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    **Constrained Minimization**[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    Method :ref:`COBYLA <optimize.minimize-cobyla>` uses the[39;49;00m
    [33m    Constrained Optimization BY Linear Approximation (COBYLA) method[39;49;00m
    [33m    [9]_, [10]_, [11]_. The algorithm is based on linear[39;49;00m
    [33m    approximations to the objective function and each constraint. The[39;49;00m
    [33m    method wraps a FORTRAN implementation of the algorithm. The[39;49;00m
    [33m    constraints functions 'fun' may return either a single number[39;49;00m
    [33m    or an array or list of numbers.[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    Method :ref:`SLSQP <optimize.minimize-slsqp>` uses Sequential[39;49;00m
    [33m    Least SQuares Programming to minimize a function of several[39;49;00m
    [33m    variables with any combination of bounds, equality and inequality[39;49;00m
    [33m    constraints. The method wraps the SLSQP Optimization subroutine[39;49;00m
    [33m    originally implemented by Dieter Kraft [12]_. Note that the[39;49;00m
    [33m    wrapper handles infinite values in bounds by converting them into[39;49;00m
    [33m    large floating values.[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    Method :ref:`trust-constr <optimize.minimize-trustconstr>` is a[39;49;00m
    [33m    trust-region algorithm for constrained optimization. It swiches[39;49;00m
    [33m    between two implementations depending on the problem definition.[39;49;00m
    [33m    It is the most versatile constrained minimization algorithm[39;49;00m
    [33m    implemented in SciPy and the most appropriate for large-scale problems.[39;49;00m
    [33m    For equality constrained problems it is an implementation of Byrd-Omojokun[39;49;00m
    [33m    Trust-Region SQP method described in [17]_ and in [5]_, p. 549. When[39;49;00m
    [33m    inequality constraints are imposed as well, it swiches to the trust-region[39;49;00m
    [33m    interior point method described in [16]_. This interior point algorithm,[39;49;00m
    [33m    in turn, solves inequality constraints by introducing slack variables[39;49;00m
    [33m    and solving a sequence of equality-constrained barrier problems[39;49;00m
    [33m    for progressively smaller values of the barrier parameter.[39;49;00m
    [33m    The previously described equality constrained SQP method is[39;49;00m
    [33m    used to solve the subproblems with increasing levels of accuracy[39;49;00m
    [33m    as the iterate gets closer to a solution.[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    **Finite-Difference Options**[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    For Method :ref:`trust-constr <optimize.minimize-trustconstr>`[39;49;00m
    [33m    the gradient and the Hessian may be approximated using[39;49;00m
    [33m    three finite-difference schemes: {'2-point', '3-point', 'cs'}.[39;49;00m
    [33m    The scheme 'cs' is, potentially, the most accurate but it[39;49;00m
    [33m    requires the function to correctly handle complex inputs and to[39;49;00m
    [33m    be differentiable in the complex plane. The scheme '3-point' is more[39;49;00m
    [33m    accurate than '2-point' but requires twice as many operations. If the[39;49;00m
    [33m    gradient is estimated via finite-differences the Hessian must be[39;49;00m
    [33m    estimated using one of the quasi-Newton strategies.[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    **Method specific options for the** `hess` **keyword**[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    +--------------+------+----------+-------------------------+-----+[39;49;00m
    [33m    | method/Hess  | None | callable | '2-point/'3-point'/'cs' | HUS |[39;49;00m
    [33m    +==============+======+==========+=========================+=====+[39;49;00m
    [33m    | Newton-CG    | x    | (n, n)   | x                       | x   |[39;49;00m
    [33m    |              |      | LO       |                         |     |[39;49;00m
    [33m    +--------------+------+----------+-------------------------+-----+[39;49;00m
    [33m    | dogleg       |      | (n, n)   |                         |     |[39;49;00m
    [33m    +--------------+------+----------+-------------------------+-----+[39;49;00m
    [33m    | trust-ncg    |      | (n, n)   | x                       | x   |[39;49;00m
    [33m    +--------------+------+----------+-------------------------+-----+[39;49;00m
    [33m    | trust-krylov |      | (n, n)   | x                       | x   |[39;49;00m
    [33m    +--------------+------+----------+-------------------------+-----+[39;49;00m
    [33m    | trust-exact  |      | (n, n)   |                         |     |[39;49;00m
    [33m    +--------------+------+----------+-------------------------+-----+[39;49;00m
    [33m    | trust-constr | x    | (n, n)   |  x                      | x   |[39;49;00m
    [33m    |              |      | LO       |                         |     |[39;49;00m
    [33m    |              |      | sp       |                         |     |[39;49;00m
    [33m    +--------------+------+----------+-------------------------+-----+[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    where LO=LinearOperator, sp=Sparse matrix, HUS=HessianUpdateStrategy[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    **Custom minimizers**[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    It may be useful to pass a custom minimization method, for example[39;49;00m
    [33m    when using a frontend to this method such as `scipy.optimize.basinhopping`[39;49;00m
    [33m    or a different library.  You can simply pass a callable as the ``method``[39;49;00m
    [33m    parameter.[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    The callable is called as ``method(fun, x0, args, **kwargs, **options)``[39;49;00m
    [33m    where ``kwargs`` corresponds to any other parameters passed to `minimize`[39;49;00m
    [33m    (such as `callback`, `hess`, etc.), except the `options` dict, which has[39;49;00m
    [33m    its contents also passed as `method` parameters pair by pair.  Also, if[39;49;00m
    [33m    `jac` has been passed as a bool type, `jac` and `fun` are mangled so that[39;49;00m
    [33m    `fun` returns just the function values and `jac` is converted to a function[39;49;00m
    [33m    returning the Jacobian.  The method shall return an `OptimizeResult`[39;49;00m
    [33m    object.[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
    [33m    The provided `method` callable must be able to accept (and possibly ignore)[39;49;00m
    [33m    arbitrary parameters; the set of parameters accepted by `minimize` may[39;49;00m
    [33m    expand in future versions and then these parameters will be passed to[39;49;00m
    [33m    the method.  You can find an example in the scipy.optimize tutorial.[39;49;00m
    [33m[39;49;00m
